From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 30 Aug 2026 20:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] linker: let a process ask for the vendor's VNDK libraries

A vendor built against an older platform needs that platform's copies of
the VNDK libraries, and a GSI's /system has moved on. On an Android 16
GSI over an Android 11 MediaTek vendor the audio HAL could not load at
all: /system/lib/libmedia_helper.so no longer carries
TypeConverter<audio_format_t>::mTable, so module-droid-card failed and
the device had no audio. Behind it were the same break in libbase
(android::base::Basename, needed by libnvram.so) and in libcutils
(thread_store_get, needed by the vendor's libhidlbase) - the whole
vendor half of the image looking at the wrong copies.

Stock Android resolves this with namespaces: /vendor gets its own, whose
link into the system image is the vndk namespace holding exactly the
libraries built for the vendor's API level. That needs an ld.config.txt,
and a Halium GSI ships none - there is not even a linkerconfig binary to
generate one, so hybris' existing HYBRIS_USE_VENDOR_NAMESPACE has
nothing to work with.

With a single namespace the two views cannot both be served: the first
copy of a soname wins the process. Scoping the preference to libraries
loaded on behalf of a /vendor caller is not enough either, because
libhardware - which hybris loads first to reach any HAL - pulls in
/system's libcutils before the vendor HAL is ever opened.

So make it the process's choice. HYBRIS_PREFER_VNDK asks for the
vendor's view, and a unit that talks to a vendor HAL sets it;
without it nothing changes. Getting this wrong either way is fatal, and
both failures are real: the compositor dies on

  cannot locate symbol "...GraphicBuffer12getDataspace..." referenced by
  "/android/system/lib/libnativewindow.so"

if it is given the APEX copies, and pulseaudio dies without them.

bionic and the LLNDK are excluded: they are the stable ABI the two
halves share, and the APEX either does not ship them or ships a copy the
/system libraries cannot use - /system/lib/liblog.so wants a
__libcpp_verbose_abort that the APEX's libc++ does not export.

The APEX is picked by the vendor's platform version, read from
/vendor/etc/selinux/plat_sepolicy_vers.txt rather than from
ro.vndk.version: an image can carry v30, v32 and v34 at once, and the
property files that name the version are 0600 root, so a service that
has dropped privileges - pulseaudio runs as "pulse" - cannot read them.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
--- a/common/q/linker.cpp
+++ b/common/q/linker.cpp
@@ -1155,6 +1155,83 @@
   return -1;
 }
 
+// The vendor -> VNDK link that ld.config.txt would have set up.
+//
+// Stock Android puts /vendor and /odm in a namespace whose only way into the
+// system image is the "vndk" namespace, so a vendor library asking for
+// libbase.so gets /apex/com.android.vndk.v<vendor>/lib/libbase.so - built for
+// the API level the vendor was built against - and never the /system copy.
+// Halium ships no ld.config.txt and hybris has a single namespace, so vendor
+// libraries were resolving against the GSI's own /system libraries instead.
+//
+// Against a recent GSI that is not a subtle difference. The MediaTek audio HAL
+// asked for libmedia_helper.so, got the /system one, which no longer carries
+// TypeConverter<audio_format_t>::mTable, and module-droid-card failed to load,
+// leaving the device with no audio; the next vendor library along, libnvram.so,
+// wanted android::base::Basename, which /system/lib's libbase.so no longer
+// exports either. That is not fixable one library at a time - it is the whole
+// vendor half of the image looking at the wrong copies.
+//
+// So when the library is needed by something under /vendor or /odm, look in
+// the matching VNDK APEX first. Everything else keeps the old order, which
+// leaves system-origin loads exactly as they were - and that matters: the APEX
+// shadows some 150 /system libraries, and preferring it wholesale breaks them
+// (/system/lib/liblog.so needs a __libcpp_verbose_abort that the APEX's libc++
+// does not export).
+static const std::string& vndk_apex_lib_dir() {
+  static std::string dir = []() -> std::string {
+    // The vendor's own VNDK version, not the newest APEX present: an image can
+    // carry v30, v32 and v34 at once and only the vendor's own one matches.
+    //
+    // Read it from the vendor's SELinux version rather than from
+    // ro.vndk.version. The property files that carry that property are 0600
+    // root, so a service which has dropped privileges cannot read them -
+    // pulseaudio runs as "pulse" - and the lookup would silently fail for
+    // exactly the process that needs it. plat_sepolicy_vers.txt is world
+    // readable, holds "<ver>.0", and states the same platform version the
+    // vendor was built against.
+    static const char* const kVersionFiles[] = {
+      "/vendor/etc/selinux/plat_sepolicy_vers.txt",
+      "/odm/etc/selinux/plat_sepolicy_vers.txt",
+    };
+
+    std::string version;
+    for (const char* version_file : kVersionFiles) {
+      FILE* f = fopen(version_file, "re");
+      if (f == nullptr) {
+        continue;
+      }
+      char line[64];
+      if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), f) != nullptr) {
+        for (const char* c = line; *c >= '0' && *c <= '9'; ++c) {
+          version.push_back(*c);
+        }
+      }
+      fclose(f);
+      if (!version.empty()) {
+        break;
+      }
+    }
+    if (version.empty()) {
+      return std::string();
+    }
+
+    std::string candidate = std::string("/apex/com.android.vndk.v") + version +
+#if defined(__LP64__)
+                            "/lib64";
+#else
+                            "/lib";
+#endif
+    char real_path[PATH_MAX];
+    if (realpath(candidate.c_str(), real_path) == nullptr) {
+      return std::string();
+    }
+    return std::string(real_path);
+  }();
+
+  return dir;
+}
+
 static int open_library(android_namespace_t* ns,
                         ZipArchiveCache* zip_archive_cache,
                         const char* name, soinfo *needed_by,
@@ -1186,6 +1263,69 @@
     return fd;
   }
 
+  // Everything except bionic and the LLNDK comes from the VNDK APEX - for the
+  // processes that ask for it with HYBRIS_PREFER_VNDK.
+  //
+  // It has to be opt-in per process. A process that talks to a vendor HAL wants
+  // the vendor's own copies of these libraries; a process that uses the GSI's
+  // own /system libraries wants those. With a single linker namespace the two
+  // cannot be served at once, and choosing wrongly is fatal either way: the
+  // compositor dies on
+  //
+  //   cannot locate symbol "...GraphicBuffer12getDataspace..." referenced by
+  //   "/android/system/lib/libnativewindow.so"
+  //
+  // if it gets the APEX copies, and pulseaudio dies on the missing
+  // TypeConverter<audio_format_t>::mTable if it does not. Stock Android has no
+  // such dilemma because vendor libraries live in their own namespace linked to
+  // vndk, which needs an ld.config.txt - a Halium GSI ships none, and no
+  // linkerconfig to generate one. Until hybris can build those namespaces
+  // itself, the unit that needs the vendor's view says so.
+  static const bool prefer_vndk = getenv("HYBRIS_PREFER_VNDK") != nullptr;
+
+  //
+  // These are the libraries stock Android resolves through the vndk namespace,
+  // and they have to match the vendor, not the GSI. The two halves have drifted
+  // far apart: /system/lib/libcutils.so no longer exports thread_store_get,
+  // which the vendor's libhidlbase calls; libmedia_helper.so has lost
+  // TypeConverter<audio_format_t>::mTable, which the MediaTek audio HAL needs;
+  // libbase.so has lost android::base::Basename, which libnvram.so needs. Each
+  // one killed module-droid-card, and the device had no audio at all.
+  //
+  // The exceptions are the libraries the vndk namespace does not own. bionic
+  // and the LLNDK are a stable ABI that vendor and system share, and the APEX
+  // either does not carry them or carries a copy that the /system libraries
+  // cannot use: /system/lib/liblog.so needs a __libcpp_verbose_abort that the
+  // APEX's libc++ does not export, so preferring the APEX for libc++ takes
+  // liblog down with it and nothing loads at all.
+  //
+  // Scoping this to libraries loaded on behalf of a /vendor caller instead
+  // sounds tidier and does not work: libhardware, which hybris loads first to
+  // reach any HAL, pulls in /system's libcutils before the vendor HAL is ever
+  // opened, and with one linker namespace the first copy of a soname wins.
+  // Stock Android escapes that by giving vendor its own namespace, which needs
+  // an ld.config.txt - a Halium GSI has none, and no linkerconfig to make one.
+  static const char* const kNeverVndk[] = {
+    "libc.so", "libm.so", "libdl.so", "liblog.so", "libc++.so", "libdl_android.so",
+    "libnativewindow.so", "libsync.so", "libvndksupport.so", "libEGL.so",
+    "libGLESv1_CM.so", "libGLESv2.so", "libGLESv3.so", "libandroid_net.so",
+  };
+  bool never_vndk = false;
+  for (const char* never : kNeverVndk) {
+    if (strcmp(name, never) == 0) {
+      never_vndk = true;
+      break;
+    }
+  }
+  if (prefer_vndk && !never_vndk && !vndk_apex_lib_dir().empty()) {
+    const std::vector<std::string> vndk_paths = { vndk_apex_lib_dir() };
+    int vndk_fd = open_library_on_paths(zip_archive_cache, name, file_offset, vndk_paths, realpath);
+    if (vndk_fd != -1) {
+      TRACE("[ opened %s from the VNDK APEX ]", name);
+      return vndk_fd;
+    }
+  }
+
   // Otherwise we try LD_LIBRARY_PATH first, and fall back to the default library path
   TRACE("[ opening %s from namespace %s from %s]", name, ns->get_name(),ns->get_ld_library_paths());
   int fd = open_library_on_paths(zip_archive_cache, name, file_offset, ns->get_ld_library_paths(), realpath);
-- 
